<div class="bhd-page__keypoints" aria-label="FAQ principles">
  <div><strong>Label first</strong><span>The current physical package label controls exact preparation and authorized conditions of use.</span></div>
  <div><strong>Current formula</strong><span>Glucose, sodium chloride, citric acid, sodium bicarbonate, potassium chloride and lemon powder.</span></div>
  <div><strong>Context matters</strong><span>Water and normal meals remain the everyday hydration baseline for many people.</span></div>
</div>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Product basics</p>
  <h2>What people most often want to know</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--3">
    <article><h3>What is Best Hydrate?</h3><p>A lemon-flavoured powdered hydration mix intended to be mixed with water according to the current package label. It combines glucose with listed electrolyte ingredients in a measured drink format.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Does it contain magnesium?</h3><p>No. Magnesium is not listed in the current formula and is not represented as a current Best Hydrate ingredient.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Is it therapeutic ORS?</h3><p>No. Best Hydrate is a consumer hydration product and should not be equated with appropriately formulated therapeutic oral rehydration solution used to treat diarrhoeal dehydration.</p></article>
    <article><h3>How do I mix it?</h3><p>Use the scoop and water quantity stated on the current package label. The label remains the controlling source if older online instructions differ.</p><a href="/pages/directions">Read Directions →</a></article>
    <article><h3>Do I need it every day?</h3><p>Not necessarily. Water and regular meals cover routine hydration for many people. A hydration mix may be useful when it fits activity, heat, heavy sweating, travel, work or personal preference.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Can I use more to hydrate faster?</h3><p>Do not assume that more powder, glucose, sodium or fluid is automatically better. Prepare the product as labelled and use individualized medical guidance where fluid or sodium intake is restricted.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__split">
  <div class="bhd-page__split-media"><img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/3768114/pexels-photo-3768114.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1500" alt="Person preparing a drink in a reusable bottle" width="1500" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div>
  <div class="bhd-page__split-copy">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Preparation</p>
    <h2>Why the current label takes priority</h2>
    <p>Packaging, serving instructions and authorized conditions of use can change over a product's life. Older webpages, screenshots or archived posts can remain discoverable long after a label changes.</p>
    <p>Best Hydrate therefore treats the current package label as the source of truth for exact scoop quantity, water volume, storage and use directions. The website explains context rather than silently preserving old instructions as permanent dosing advice.</p>
    <a class="bhd-page__text-link" href="/pages/directions">Current preparation guidance →</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__prose-grid">
  <article class="bhd-page__prose">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Choosing the right context</p>
    <h2>Hydration is not one problem with one answer</h2>
    <p>A long endurance event, a hot work shift, an ordinary office day, rehabilitation and clinical dehydration are different situations. The amount and type of fluid that makes sense can differ substantially between them.</p>
    <p>Use the application pages to match the hydration discussion to the situation instead of treating the same advice as universal.</p>
  </article>
  <aside class="bhd-page__callout"><span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Need medical advice?</span><h3>Individual restrictions override general website guidance.</h3><p>People with prescribed fluid, sodium, carbohydrate or electrolyte restrictions should follow their clinician's plan.</p></aside>
</section>

